.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-admin-health - Operator diagnostics: one verdict, then a check per thing that can be wrong


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 admin health [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
The gateway's operator diagnostics: a verdict ("ok", "degraded" or "error") and a named
check per standing failure condition — the worker fleet, the indexer's intake, stuck
transactions, open webhook circuits, balance divergences — each with its figures and a
sentence saying what they mean.

.PP
--rpc asks the other question: can the gateway reach the chains. Every active chain's RPC
endpoints are probed from the gateway's own process, with each one's status, head block
and latency.

.PP
The exit code follows the verdict: 0 when it is "ok", 1 otherwise, after the report is
printed. So 'rail0 admin health -q || alert' works as a check.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for health

.PP
\fB--rpc\fP[=false]
	Probe every active chain's RPC endpoints from the gateway instead of reading the diagnostics


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 admin health
  rail0 admin health --rpc
  rail0 admin health --pretty
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-admin(1)\fP
